From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 16 Aug 2026 12:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] boot: say what actually breaks the base package rule today

The note naming the non-conforming ports is from 2011 and has drifted.
It is still right that m68k-amiga puts chipset code in graphics.library,
but the PowerPC half now points at the wrong module: the display driver
setup kludge moved to dosboot.resource, where rom/dosboot/menu.c defines
INITHIDDS_KLUDGE for __ppc__ and arch/ppc-chrp/dosboot and
arch/ppc-sam440/dosboot supply an InitBootConfig() naming radeon.hidd.
Nothing in dos.library does this any more.

Two more cases were never listed. arch/m68k-amiga/hidd/gfx replaces
gfx.hidd's rgbconv.c, and nothing in that file is machine-specific -- it
registers four colour conversions rather than all of them, which is a ROM
size trade, and the cpu-specific equivalents for i386 and x86_64 are
already filed under arch/<cpu>-all. And the m68k assembly for
LockLayerRom(), UnlockLayerRom() and AttemptLockLayerRom() touches no
hardware at all, so it is cpu-specific code filed under a machine.

Naming the functions rather than the module makes the remaining work
countable: six functions in graphics.library reach $DFF000, and the rest
of what is filed as machine-specific is not.

Also record what these cases have in common, since it is the thing that
keeps reintroducing them: the gates are on the CPU. defined(mc68000) and
__ppc__ say nothing about which machine the module will run on, and a
base module that has to reach hardware wants a hook whose generic
implementation does nothing -- rom/graphics/graphics_arch.c and
rom/hidds/gfx/rgbconv_arch.c both work that way.
---
 boot/modular_kickstart.txt | 33 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
diff --git a/boot/modular_kickstart.txt b/boot/modular_kickstart.txt
index 4725ee9500..cd8ce25a1f 100644
--- a/boot/modular_kickstart.txt
+++ b/boot/modular_kickstart.txt
@@ -47,13 +47,40 @@ functional difference between ELF and PKG forms.
  lddemon.resource
 
   These modules are not allowed to have machine-specific code because it
-violates portability convention. As of 12.09.2011, m68k-amiga
-(chipset-specific code in graphics.library) and PowerPC-native (display
-driver setup kludge in dos.library boot code) ports do not conform to this rule.
+violates portability convention. This was first recorded as broken on
+12.09.2011 for m68k-amiga and PowerPC-native. As of 16.08.2026 the
+following still do not conform:
+
+ - graphics.library on m68k-amiga. VBeamPos(), WaitBlit(), BltClear(),
+   MoveSprite(), ChangeExtSpriteA() and SetChipRev() are replaced from
+   arch/m68k-amiga/graphics and address the chipset at $DFF000 directly.
+   The same directory also replaces LockLayerRom(), UnlockLayerRom() and
+   AttemptLockLayerRom() with m68k assembly that touches no hardware --
+   that is cpu-specific code filed under a machine, and belongs in
+   arch/m68k-all.
+
+ - gfx.hidd on m68k-amiga. arch/m68k-amiga/hidd/gfx replaces rgbconv.c
+   with a build registering four colour conversions instead of all of
+   them. Nothing in it is machine-specific; it is a ROM size trade, and
+   the cpu-specific equivalents for i386 and x86_64 are filed under
+   arch/<cpu>-all as the rule allows.
+
+ - dosboot.resource on PowerPC. This is where the display driver setup
+   kludge went; it is no longer in dos.library. rom/dosboot/menu.c
+   defines INITHIDDS_KLUDGE for __ppc__ and calls initHidds(), and
+   arch/ppc-chrp/dosboot and arch/ppc-sam440/dosboot supply the
+   InitBootConfig() that names radeon.hidd.
 
   Of course "machine-specific code" does not include "cpu-specific code".
 It's allowed for any module to have relevant portions in arch/<cpu>-all.
 
+  Note that a gate on the CPU is not a gate on the machine: defined(mc68000)
+and __ppc__ say nothing about which machine the module will end up running
+on. Where a base module has to reach hardware, the hardware belongs behind a
+hook whose generic implementation does nothing, replaced from arch/ for the
+machine that has it. rom/graphics/graphics_arch.c and
+rom/hidds/gfx/rgbconv_arch.c are the two examples in the tree.
+
   This package is described in rom/mmakefile.src.
 
 2. FS
